content.text:
**Yes for the contract — Modal didn’t need Daytona-style code edits.**

Modal only implements lifecycle **`terminate`**, and it only reads `request.wait`:

```124:133:sdks/typescript/providers/modal/src/modal.ts
  async terminate(id: string, request: TerminateOptions): Promise<ExitResult> {
    try {
      const sandbox = await this.native.sandboxes.fromId(id);
      if (request.wait === true)
        return { exitCode: await sandbox.terminate({ wait: true }) };
      await sandbox.terminate();
      return {};
```

It never converted protobuf `Duration` → seconds (unlike Daytona’s `start`/`stop`/…).

What still applies to Modal:

| Change | Modal |
|--------|--------|
| `TerminateOptions.timeout` is now `number` (ms) | Covered via shared types in `@sandbox-kit/sandbox` |
| Client no longer passes wire `Duration` | Covered — Modal gets the same ms request object |
| Provider code changes for this smell | **None needed** |

So Modal is fixed at the boundary; only Daytona needed the `secondsFromMs` adapter update. The earlier Modal change (drop redundant `validateConfig`) was a different smell and is already in the PR.

